text=['# Corpus coverage and evidenced lessons','', 'Study date: 2026-10-01, America/Toronto. Scope: all current charts labeled IN16 and AT17, with creation/rechart dates 2023-10-01–2026-10-01 prioritized.','', 'Acquired 53 official-formatVersion3 charts: 38 IN16 and 15 AT17. All chart requests and all corresponding full song audio requests succeeded. Canonical line-local arrangement is separately plotted from presented contacts; normalization collapses approach axes but does not by itself solve cross-line hand assignment. Each resource has URL, byte count and SHA256 in `../corpus/manifest.json`. GitHub downloader: https://github.com/swordalt/phigros-chart-downloader ; UI: https://swordalt.github.io/phigros-chart-downloader/ ; actual resources: https://github.com/7aGiven/Phigros_Resource . Direct read-only requests avoided website download telemetry.','', 'The broad wiki chart list was stale at 36 IN16 +13 AT17. The two additional songs in v4.0.0 are Entrance to the Chaos and Exoplanetary Mirage, each with IN and AT. Their individual wiki unlock pages confirm they were playable in the Sep25 release; the Oct2 continuation is later content. See https://wikiwiki.jp/phigros/Chapter%209%20%E9%9A%A0%E3%81%97%E6%9B%B2%204 and https://wikiwiki.jp/phigros/Chapter%209%20%E9%9A%A0%E3%81%97%E6%9B%B2%205 .','', '33 of these charts have a creation or explicitly identified rechart in the requested three-year interval. Dates use https://wikiwiki.jp/phigros/アップデート履歴/3 and chart-origin versions from https://wikiwiki.jp/phigros/高レベル譜面定数(CC)表 . The v3.11.0 level-label promotion is NOT treated as a newly made chart. Twelve pre-v3 origin/rechart dates remain exact-day-unverified, but are known to predate the three-year cutoff. Chronostasis and Lyrith are recent remakes despite older song introductions. Cycle of Redemption normal IN was introduced 2025-08-31; its 2024 April Fools SP is a different chart.','', 'Every acquired chart was scanned in full numerically and inspected as a static schematic atlas (18 sheets, all 53 rows). Six snapshots plus the whole contact timeline are available in `atlas.html`. This is not a claim that every chart was watched in full gameplay, played, or validated by a human top player. The atlas uses official linear event transforms, speed-floor integration and a 16:9 sample; hold snapshot glyphs have short schematic tails; timeline tails mark actual duration at initial head x and do not trace moving held contacts. It does not model the complete official hit engine.','', 'One explicit source discrepancy remains: Stardust:RAY IN is 1382 in both wiki aggregate and individual page, while the acquired current resource contains 1392 raw note objects. No assumption is made about why. Other 49 aggregate-confirmed charts match wiki note count; the four v4.0.0 charts match their individual pages.','', '## Four note roles and difficulty','', '- Official JSON types: 1 Tap, 2 Drag, 3 Hold, 4 Flick. Tap articulates a discrete strike; Drag judges contact along a sequence without a new press per object; Hold creates continuous ownership over a duration; Flick adds motion commitment/accent. Spatial proximity and judgment windows mean these are not four interchangeable colored taps.','- Distorted Fate IN: 1449 = 841 Tap, 430 Drag, 132 Hold, 46 Flick; AT: 1283 = 907 Tap, 115 Drag, 212 Hold, 49 Flick. AT has fewer objects but more strike/sustain ownership and 46 line objects versus24.','- Rrhar’il IN and AT both1300. IN: 802/410/53/35; AT: 991/44/123/142. Same total, very different physical articulation.','- Toy Rhapsody IN1418 versus AT1374: IN285/637/318/178; AT652/197/317/208. Again the AT reduces drag mass and makes more independent attacks.','- Verrückt IN: 837 Tap,398 Drag,50 Hold,0 Flick. Quality does not require all four types.','- Raw onset groups are not finger counts. A drag ribbon can contain many objects judged by one continuous touch. Conversely one new Tap during several held contacts can demand an additional free finger even with a small onset group. `raw_note_objects_plus_active_holds_peak` is deliberately named as a raw object statistic and is not a finger-capacity estimate. A practical fingering review needs spatial contact merging, hold tracking, hand travel, fresh press/release timing, crossing and occlusion.','', '## Aesthetic and compositional lessons','', '- Judge-line motion is both stage geometry and a playable coordinate transform. Decorative line objects often have no notes; line/event counts must not be read as simultaneous contact demand or aesthetic quality. Sampled easing exports can create thousands of tiny events for one authored movement.','- Broad recurring contrasts are stable stations versus traveling diagonals; dense strike fields versus sustained ownership; ornamental drag contour versus keyboard detail; symmetric construction followed by a deliberate asymmetry. These are visual observations of the atlas, not music-source attribution.','- IN→AT often changes how voices are owned, rather than uniformly increasing speed, density or moving-line chaos. The lower drag share makes individual strikes, holding fingers and flick commitments matter.','- Rest intervals frequently keep a few sustained owned voices. This creates breathing room and preserves continuity, then makes the return of fast detail meaningful. Removing those intervals would flatten the arrangement and physical arc.','- A 4K stair must be planned as an ordered lane/hand pattern with an entry finger, an exit/repeat decision and enough release time. Dense arbitrary left-to-right taps are not enough. Crossing an owned hold lane, striking through a drag carpet or interleaving a flick changes the required solution.','- Whole-chart templates should preserve phrase boundaries, repeated motif identity and a planned climax. Theme transfer should reuse a transformation rule (mirroring, orbital motion, frames, branching) at chosen musical anchors; it should not copy an entire official chart’s events and simply change the song.','', '## Per-chart static review','']
